// A pram or a wheelchair, on top of foot.json + foot_elevation.json.
//
// `max_slope` rather than `average_slope`: a path that averages 5 % is no use
// if it does so with a 15 % ramp in the middle. It is signed and flips with
// direction, so every band below names both signs — a descent with a loaded
// pram is a braking problem, not a free ride. The import runs long edge
// sampling for this; without it `max_slope` collapses to `average_slope` on any
// way OSM maps with no intermediate nodes, which is most of the long ones.
//
// ## Why a band per percent rather than a curve
//
// A gradient this gradual wants a continuous penalty, and 11.0 cannot express
// one. `ValueExpressionVisitor` accepts only number literals, a *single*
// encoded value, unary `-`, parentheses, `Math.sqrt` (`allowedMethods` is
// literally `Set.of("sqrt")`, `allowedMethodParents` `Set.of("Math")`), and the
// binary operators `*`, `+`, `-`. There is no `/`, no `Math.abs`, and
// `.ordinal()` carries an explicit TODO as unsupported. `edge.getDistance()`
// exists only on master.
//
// The killer is the range check: `findVariables` evaluates the expression at
// `getMinStorableDecimal()` and `getMaxOrMaxStorableDecimal()` — nothing else —
// and rejects a negative result, never consulting the enclosing `if`. For
// `max_slope` those endpoints are -31 and 31, so a decreasing `a - b * slope`
// needs `a >= 31b`; at `a = 1` the coefficient caps at 1/31 and 12 % still
// scores 0.61. With one encoded value and no `abs`, symmetry in |slope| is
// unreachable anyway.
//
// So the curve is tabulated instead. `max_slope` is stored with factor 1, i.e.
// whole percent, so a band per percent is exactly the resolution the data has —
// finer would be inventing precision, coarser would throw some away. 8 % is
// where the accessibility standards put a ramp's limit and lands at 0.52; 12 %
// is where a loaded pram stops going up at all and lands at 0.08.
//
// Nothing is a hard block, because slope here is DEM-derived and one bad cell
// should cost a detour rather than sever the graph — 0.01 is entered only when
// there is no alternative at all.
//
// ## Smoothness and surface
//
// Both are graded rather than lumped, because `bad` and `impassable` are not
// the same claim. OSM defines the scale by what can use the way, and
// `intermediate` is the rung that names a wheelchair explicitly — so that is
// where a pram stops being unremarkable, and `impassable` ("no wheeled
// vehicle") is the one value that earns an outright block. Like STEPS it is a
// tag rather than a measurement, which is what makes a 0 honest there.
//
// The two compound: `surface=ground` with `smoothness=bad` lands at 0.25 × 0.4.
// That is deliberate — upstream avoids double-penalising for bike *speed*
// (#3302), but for a pram a rough surface and a rough tread really are two
// separate problems.
////
// ## Why nothing here goes below ~0.06
//
// The three ladders multiply, and this is a CH profile. `CustomWeighting` gives
// `weight = seconds / priority`, and `CHStorage` stores a shortcut weight as a
// scaled int capped at `MAX_WEIGHT = (Integer.MAX_VALUE << 1) / 1000`, about
// 4.29e6; past that `weightFromDouble` counts `numShortcutsExceedingWeight` and
// returns the infinity marker, whose own comment warns of
// connection-not-found. An earlier draft of this file floored at 0.01 x 0.02 x
// 0.04 = 8e-6 with a 2 km/h speed cap — 225 000 weight per metre, overflowing a
// shortcut after nineteen metres. The floors below keep the worst compounded
// product at 0.06 x 0.08 x 0.15 = 7.2e-4, which at the 3 km/h floor is ~1670 per
// metre and so survives a 2.5 km shortcut — the same headroom the previous
// two-tier model had.
//
// That headroom is now halved from outside this file. foot_carriageway.json
// stacks onto this profile and multiplies by as little as 0.5 on primary, so the
// worst compounded product is 3.6e-4, ~3340 per metre, and a shortcut survives
// about 1.25 km rather than 2.5 km. Still far from the 19 m of the draft that
// failed, but the margin is no longer what the paragraph above assumed, and it
// shrinks again if that ladder is ever deepened. Check
// `numShortcutsExceedingWeight` in the import log before lowering anything in
// either file.
//
// A hard 0 is *not* the same hazard: infinity excludes the edge from
// contraction outright, which is why STEPS and `impassable` stay at 0.
//
// The speed block is about arrival times, not route choice. foot.json gives
// every surface walking pace and foot_elevation.json only slows for gradient,
// so without it a pram crosses sand as fast as it crosses asphalt.
//
// Nothing here about `hike_rating`: foot.json already blocks anything at 2 or
// above, which is every value a pram would care about.
{
  "priority": [
    { "if": "road_class == STEPS", "multiply_by": "0" },

    { "if": "max_slope > 15 || max_slope < -15", "multiply_by": "0.06" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 14 || max_slope < -14", "multiply_by": "0.07" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 13 || max_slope < -13", "multiply_by": "0.09" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 12 || max_slope < -12", "multiply_by": "0.11" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 11 || max_slope < -11", "multiply_by": "0.16" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 10 || max_slope < -10", "multiply_by": "0.24" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 9 || max_slope < -9", "multiply_by": "0.34" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 8 || max_slope < -8", "multiply_by": "0.46" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 7 || max_slope < -7", "multiply_by": "0.60" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 6 || max_slope < -6", "multiply_by": "0.73" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 5 || max_slope < -5", "multiply_by": "0.83" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 4 || max_slope < -4", "multiply_by": "0.90" },
    { "else_if": "max_slope > 3 || max_slope < -3", "multiply_by": "0.95" },

    { "if": "smoothness == IMPASSABLE", "multiply_by": "0" },
    { "else_if": "smoothness == VERY_HORRIBLE", "multiply_by": "0.08" },
    { "else_if": "smoothness == HORRIBLE", "multiply_by": "0.15" },
    { "else_if": "smoothness == VERY_BAD", "multiply_by": "0.3" },
    { "else_if": "smoothness == BAD", "multiply_by": "0.5" },
    { "else_if": "smoothness == INTERMEDIATE", "multiply_by": "0.9" },

    { "if": "surface == SAND", "multiply_by": "0.15" },
    { "else_if": "surface == GRASS", "multiply_by": "0.25" },
    { "else_if": "surface == GROUND || surface == DIRT", "multiply_by": "0.4" },
    { "else_if": "surface == COBBLESTONE || surface == GRAVEL", "multiply_by": "0.45" },
    { "else_if": "surface == FINE_GRAVEL", "multiply_by": "0.75" },
    { "else_if": "surface == COMPACTED || surface == WOOD", "multiply_by": "0.85" },
    { "else_if": "surface == PAVING_STONES", "multiply_by": "0.95" }
  ],
  "speed": [
    { "if": "surface == SAND || surface == GRASS", "limit_to": "3" },
    {
      "else_if": "surface == GROUND || surface == DIRT || surface == COBBLESTONE || surface == GRAVEL",
      "limit_to": "3"
    },
    { "else_if": "surface == FINE_GRAVEL", "limit_to": "4" },
    { "if": "smoothness == VERY_BAD || smoothness == HORRIBLE || smoothness == VERY_HORRIBLE", "limit_to": "3" },
    { "else_if": "smoothness == BAD", "limit_to": "3.5" }
  ]
}
